; host/lazy_seq.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.LazySeq`.
;
; The lazy sequence: a recipe, run at most once per node, whose successful
; answer is shared, whose known failures may be retried, and whose recursive
; force, cyclic retained value and lost evaluator are terminal. This file is the
; `.bl` port of `lib/beam_lisp/lazy_seq.ex`'s `BeamLisp.LazySeq` half, compiled
; by `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into ONE exact-named beam whose
; `__struct__/0,1` pair and `__info__/1` are SYNTHESIZED by the `:struct_fields`
; option in the live module's declaration order (`:resource`, `:metadata`).
;
; THE PRODUCTION TAG IS SPELLED LITERALLY, never read from a fun literal. The
; native memo graph recognizes a lazy node by its struct tag:
; `BeamLisp.LazyMemo.dependencies/1` descends into
; `%{__struct__: BeamLisp.LazySeq, resource: resource}`, and the cycle guard is
; built on that walk. A value tagged with a probe atom is an OPAQUE map there:
; its memo resource is neither edge-shared nor cycle-checked, so a probe compile
; that minted its own tag would answer DIFFERENT cycle/retry behaviour and prove
; nothing. `new/1,2` therefore builds `%BeamLisp.LazySeq{}` at any compile target
; (the `reactive.bl` `Derived`-tag idiom), and every tag test below compares
; against that same literal. The module-exact fun-literal read is used only to
; name THIS beam in the exception it raises for a bad argument.
;
; THE CELL HOLDS THE RECIPE (perf/lazy-engine, PLAN-151 W5). A node's cell state
; is one of `{:pending thunk}` (or `{:pending thunk recipe}`, see THE FOLD
; RECIPE), `{:running owner attempt waiters}`, `{:completed value}`,
; `{:failed thunk kind reason stack}` or `{:terminal reason}`. The struct is
; `resource metadata`: a handle into that cell plus the value-owned metadata.
; The THUNK (and the fold recipe) is on the cell — it is the recipe, and the
; state that owns it keeps it alive. The running state does NOT carry it: its
; owner is executing it, and every other process reaches the outcome through the
; attempt's reply. `memo_state/1` reports the running cell in the RECALLED
; 5-tuple shape (`{:running nil owner attempt waiters}`) so callers keep the
; logical view.
;
; WHY THE THUNK IS NOT A STRUCT FIELD. A produced value can EMBED a node:
; `chain/2` builds `[e | %LazySeq{…}]`. A closure in the struct would make every
; deep copy of such a value walk the closure's free variables — and for a
; realised chain those hold the previous node, whose own thunk holds the one
; before it. The struct's `resource` is ONE word, so the cell copy, the CAS
; comparison and `estimate_bytes/1`'s `flat_size` all stop at it.
;
; ONE WALK AND ONE COPY IN, ONE COPY OUT. Realising one element costs exactly:
; one walk of the thunk's captured environment (at create — those are the cell's
; cycle edges), one copy of it into the cell (create), one copy out (the claimer
; reads `{:pending thunk}`), plus the value's own walk and copy when it is
; published. Everything else is O(1): a claim, a waiter registration and a
; terminal transition are VERSION compare-and-swaps carrying `:keep` — no term
; load, no term compare, no dependency walk, no graph lock (see
; `BeamLisp.LazyMemo`).
;
; THE FAILURE IS RECORDED BIT-FOR-BIT. The live `evaluate_attempt` catches
; `kind, reason` in two variables and stores `{:failed, thunk, kind, reason,
; __STACKTRACE__}`; a waiter replays it with `:erlang.raise(kind, reason,
; frames)`. A plain `catch` NORMALIZES what it caught — an `:error` becomes an
; exception struct, a throw of a non-exception an ExInfo, an `:exit` a
; `{:exit, value}` pair — which is right for user code and lossy for a cell that
; must RECORD and REPLAY a failure unchanged. `(catch-raw [kind reason stack]
; …)` is the branch's clause for exactly that (PLAN-151, carried with this
; wave): the class atom, the unnormalized reason and the raw stack.
;
; ## THE FOLD RECIPE
;
; A producer that can also compute its elements from its SOURCE may attach a
; fold `recipe` to its node. A reducing consumer calls it INSTEAD of the cell
; walk, and it creates no cells at all:
;
;     recipe :: (fn [rf init] -> acc | BeamLisp.Reduced)
;
; `rf` is a raw 2-arity Erlang fun `(rf acc x)`. Only a node whose head cell is
; still `{:pending thunk recipe}` may fold from source, and a reducing consumer
; must CLAIM that recipe before running it (`claim_recipe`): the cell goes
; `:running` under the consumer, so a concurrent force — or a concurrent fold —
; shares the one attempt instead of running the producer a second time. A fold
; that runs to the end publishes the complete element list as the node's
; `{:completed …}` state (`complete_attempt`) — Clojure's realise-once contract.
; A fold the consumer HALTS produced only a prefix, publishes nothing, and hands
; the cell back `:pending` (`release_recipe`) so a later full read still folds
; from the start. Once ANYTHING is realised the memo walk is used instead, and a
; fold that lost the claim race reads the fulfilled memo (or waits on the
; winner) rather than re-running the producer.
;
; ## THE CACHE BUDGET (PLAN-154 B)
;
; `:lazy_cache_budget_bytes` (default 512 MiB) bounds the bytes a lazy VALUE
; publication retains off-heap. Every state has a measured cost — one cell's
; native overhead plus `estimate_bytes/1` (a `:running`/`:terminal` control
; state is a constant 64; a `:pending` recipe, a `:completed` value and a
; `:failed` class/reason/frame record are measured) — and a publication is
; charged the DIFFERENCE it makes, so replacing a large state with a smaller one
; is free.
;
; The ADMITTED publications are the fold's claim (`claim_recipe`), its
; completion or failure (`complete_attempt`/`fail_attempt`), its halt release
; (`release_recipe`), the attempt's `publish_attempt`, and the pending → running
; claim. They go through `LazyMemo/exchange_version_admitted`, which reserves
; the growth atomically against `retained + reserved` and commits or releases it
; with the state. A fold claim is a SHRINK (the constant-size running state
; replaces the measured pending recipe) and the release that undoes it restores
; exactly the state the claim replaced, so the pair is retained-neutral: a fold
; and the equivalent force decide IDENTICALLY, and two publishers that each
; start below the ceiling cannot jointly exceed it. The two constant-size
; control transitions that can grow NOTHING — a waiter registration
; (`wait_for_attempt`) and the owner-loss terminal (`owner_lost`) — stay on the
; unadmitted exchange: a resource verdict there could only strand a waiter.
;
; A refused publication never strands an attempt AND never leaves one
; `:pending` to be recomputed. Every path that holds a claim — the fold's
; completion, the fold's halt release, the ordinary attempt — turns the refusal
; into a TERMINAL budget-refusal state: a constant-size replacement that is
; admitted at any budget, wakes every registered waiter with the owner's own
; reason, and makes a later read fail fast with the same reason rather than
; re-running a producer that already ran.
;
; THE FOLD RECIPE HELPERS ARE TOP LEVEL, like the force protocol. A pinned module
; is functions-only and EVERY top-level `defn` is exported; the recalled
; module's private helpers would normally be LOCAL closures, but a call through a
; closure is an INDIRECT call, and on this host an indirect call costs up to ten
; times a direct one. `force/1` runs on every realised element, so its whole
; protocol is compiled as direct calls. The PUBLIC ABI is unchanged; the extra
; names are internal and their docstrings say so.
;
; THE BOOT EDGE. `lazy-seq` is a core macro that expands to
; `(BeamLisp.LazySeq/from_fun (fn [] …))`, so `new`/`from_fun` run while the
; boot tier is loading. Their HAPPY path touches only Erlang BIFs, `Map`,
; `BeamLisp.LazyMemo` and the native resource; the tuple for the pending recipe
; is built with `erlang/make_tuple` + `erlang/setelement`, never BL `tuple`, and
; the realize error message with `erlang/iolist_to_binary`, never BL `str`. The
; walks' `raw-cons` resolves directly to a boot-tier body module the caller has
; already loaded.
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_lazy_seq_test.bl

(ns host.lazy-seq)

(defn lazy?
  "True for a lazy node — a map tagged with the production `BeamLisp.LazySeq`
   struct. Any other value, nil included, is false."
  [x]
  (and (erlang/is_map x)
       (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.LazySeq" (maps/get :__struct__ x nil))))

(defn chunk_size
  "The chunk width a producer copies per realization step."
  []
  32)

(defn new
  "Wrap a zero-arity recipe in a shared lazy node. A successful answer is
   memoized; a failure is stored as a retryable state. The `{:pending, thunk}`
   tuple is built with Erlang BIFs so the boot edge reaches no BL runtime helper.
   A non-zero-arity thunk is the `FunctionClauseError` the live guard raised.

   The two-arity form also gives the node a fold `recipe` a reducing consumer
   may use while it is still `:pending` (see the namespace header). The recipe
   lives ON THE CELL, `{:pending, thunk, recipe}`, for the reason the thunk does:
   a struct field would be copied with every value that embeds the node."
  ([thunk]
    (let [own (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))]
      (if (erlang/is_function thunk 0)
        (maps/put :metadata nil
          (maps/put :resource
            (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create
              (erlang/setelement 2 (erlang/make_tuple 2 :pending) thunk))
            (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.LazySeq" (maps/new))))
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
            (list (tuple :module own) (tuple :function :new) (tuple :arity 1)))))))
  ([thunk recipe]
    (let [own (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module))]
      (if (and (erlang/is_function thunk 0)
               (erlang/is_function recipe)
               (erlang/== 2 (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info recipe :arity))))
        (maps/put :metadata nil
          (maps/put :resource
            (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/create
              (erlang/setelement 3
                (erlang/setelement 2 (erlang/make_tuple 3 :pending) thunk)
                recipe))
            (maps/put :__struct__ :"Elixir.BeamLisp.LazySeq" (maps/new))))
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
            (list (tuple :module own) (tuple :function :new) (tuple :arity 2))))))))

(defn from_fun
  "`new/1` under the name the `lazy-seq` core macro expands to. A non-function
   argument is the `FunctionClauseError` the live guard raised."
  [thunk]
  (if (erlang/is_function thunk 0)
    (new thunk)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :from_fun)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn input
  "Normalize a producer input: a vector is unwrapped and reclassified; a list
   longer than one chunk becomes a native `SeqCursor`, a shorter list stays a
   list; anything else is itself. `length` refusing an improper list answers 0,
   exactly as the live `rescue ArgumentError` did."
  [x]
  (cond
    (and (erlang/is_map x) (erlang/== :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Vector" (maps/get :__struct__ x nil)))
      (input (BeamLisp.Vector/to_list x))

    (erlang/is_list x)
      (let [size
            (try
              (erlang/length x)
              (catch e
                (if (erlang/== :"Elixir.ArgumentError" (maps/get :__struct__ e nil))
                  0
                  (erlang/apply :erlang :raise (list :error e __STACKTRACE__)))))]
        (if (erlang/> size 32) (BeamLisp.SeqCursor/new x) x))

    :else x))

(defn chain
  "Prepend `elems` to a node produced by `tail_fun`: one shared tail node, each
   element a raw cons onto it. The empty `elems` answers nil whatever `tail_fun`
   is, as the live first clause did; a non-zero-arity tail is the live
   `FunctionClauseError`."
  [elems tail_fun]
  (cond
    (and (erlang/is_list elems) (erlang/== elems ())) nil

    (erlang/is_function tail_fun 0)
      (erlang/apply :lists :foldr
        (list (fn [e acc] (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/raw-cons e acc)) (new tail_fun) elems))

    :else
      (erlang/error
        (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
          (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
                (tuple :function :chain)
                (tuple :arity 2))))))

; ── the fold recipe ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn pending_recipe
  "`{recipe version}` when the node's head cell is `{:pending thunk recipe}` at
   `version`, else nil: no recipe, or already claimed, realised, failed or
   terminal. The version is what `publish_folded` CASes against.

   A CONSTANT-SIZE PEEK answers the dominant non-pending case first (PLAN-154
   D): the SECOND fold of a realised node learns it is `:completed` from an atom
   and an integer instead of exporting the whole published value onto the heap
   to discover the same thing. Only a `:pending` tag pays for the full versioned
   read, and that read must agree with the peeked version — the peek is not a
   snapshot, and a version that moved means the pending state the recipe
   belonged to is gone."
  [l]
  (let [resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        peeked (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/peek resource)]
    ; A pinned host resolves NO namespace var, so the tests are stated
    ; positively: `(not x)` would compile to `Env.fetch!("host.lazy-seq", "not")`
    ; and a cold VM (which never interns that ns) would fail on it.
    (cond
      (erlang/== :pending (erlang/element 1 peeked))
      (let [current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
            version (erlang/element 1 current)
            state (erlang/element 2 current)]
        (cond
          (erlang/== version (erlang/element 2 peeked))
          (cond
            (and (erlang/is_tuple state)
                 (erlang/== 3 (erlang/tuple_size state))
                 (erlang/== :pending (erlang/element 1 state)))
            (tuple (erlang/element 3 state) version)
            :else nil)
          :else nil))
      :else nil)))

(defn pending?
  "Whether the node's head cell is still `:pending` — the one state in which a
   fold recipe may be used (see the namespace header). Any other state — already
   realised, in flight elsewhere, failed-then-retried, or terminal — reads
   through the memo walk instead.

   One constant-size peek answers it (PLAN-154 D): no state is exported, so a
   node holding a large published value costs an atom to interrogate."
  [l]
  (cond
    (lazy? l)
    (erlang/== :pending
      (erlang/element 1 (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/peek (erlang/map_get :resource l))))
    :else
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :pending?)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn claim_recipe
  "INTERNAL: claim a node's pending fold `recipe` for THIS process, before any
   of the recipe's effects run.

   A fold produces the SAME node a force produces, so it takes the SAME
   ownership: the versioned `:running` state — owner, attempt, waiters —
   published by the admitted compare-exchange `claim_attempt` uses. There is NO
   second ownership registry. A concurrent force (or fold) that reaches the cell
   while this claim is held finds a running attempt, registers as a waiter, and
   is answered by `publish_attempt` (or re-dispatched by `release_recipe`),
   exactly as it would be for a force.

   Answers `{:claimed attempt pending}` when this process won, where `pending`
   is the `{:pending thunk recipe}` state the claim replaced — the halt path
   hands it back through `release_recipe`. Answers nil when there is nothing to
   claim: no recipe, a cell already realised/claimed/failed/terminal, or one
   that published between the peek and the compare-exchange (whose `:retry`
   means exactly that). Either way the caller still reaches a correct answer —
   the memo walk reads the fulfilled value, or waits on the winner.

   A claim the cache ceiling refuses raises
   `ForceError({:lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget})` BEFORE the recipe runs:
   the same verdict, at the same point, as the ordinary force path."
  [l]
  (if (lazy? l)
    (let [resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
          peeked (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/peek resource)]
      ; Positively stated for the same reason `pending_recipe` is: a pinned host
      ; must resolve no namespace var (`not` compiles to an `Env.fetch!`).
      (cond
        (erlang/== :pending (erlang/element 1 peeked))
        (let [current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
              version (erlang/element 1 current)
              state (erlang/element 2 current)]
          (cond
            (erlang/== version (erlang/element 2 peeked))
            (cond
              (and (erlang/is_tuple state)
                   (erlang/== 3 (erlang/tuple_size state))
                   (erlang/== :pending (erlang/element 1 state)))
              (do
                (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/admit!)
                (let [budget (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/cache_budget)
                      attempt (erlang/make_ref)
                      running (tuple :running (erlang/self) attempt ())
                      outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version_admitted
                                resource version running :keep budget)]
                  (cond
                    (erlang/== :ok outcome) (tuple :claimed attempt state)
                    (erlang/== :retry outcome) nil
                    (erlang/== :over_budget outcome)
                    (force_error (tuple :lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget))
                    :else (force_error :cyclic_memo))))
              :else nil)
            :else nil))
        :else nil))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :claim_recipe)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn publish_folded
  "Publish the COMPLETE element list a fold recipe produced over the node's
   pending head cell at `version` — the recipe's answer becomes the shared
   answer, so a later consumer reads it instead of re-running the producer
   (Clojure's realise-once contract). A version that moved or a would-be cycle
   leaves the cell as it is: publishing is an optimisation of the NEXT read,
   never needed for this fold's own answer.

   THE PUBLICATION IS ADMITTED (PLAN-154 B). The completed list is the fold
   path's whole retained cost, so it is reserved against the cache ceiling
   atomically with the commit — bypassing the gate here is what let a fold
   succeed under a budget under which the equivalent force was refused.

   UNCLAIMED, AND NO LONGER THE FOLD PATH. A publication at a version the caller
   read is not an OWNERSHIP claim: two folds could both read the pending version
   and both run the producer, and a refusal leaves the node `:pending` for the
   next consumer to recompute. The reducing consumers therefore go through
   `claim_recipe` + `complete_attempt`, which claim the cell first and turn a
   refusal into the terminal budget state. This door stays because the pinned
   ABI pins it (`pinned-abi-surface`), and a direct caller still gets the exact
   versioned CAS and the admitted refusal.

   Answers `:ok`, `:retry` or `:cycle`, and raises `ForceError` on refusal."
  [l version elems]
  (let [state (tuple :completed (cond (erlang/== elems ()) nil :else elems))
        budget (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/cache_budget)
        answer (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version_admitted
                 (erlang/map_get :resource l) version state
                 (attempt_deps state) budget)]
    (cond
      (erlang/== :over_budget answer)
      (force_error (tuple :lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget))
      :else answer)))

; ── the force protocol ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; INTERNAL, AND TOP LEVEL FOR A MEASURED REASON. A call through a local closure
; is an INDIRECT call, and on this host an indirect call costs up to ten times a
; direct one. `force/1` runs on every realised element, so its whole protocol
; lives at top level — the recalled module's own private names, one level up —
; and the dispatch between them is a direct call.
;
; `again` is the caller's FRESH REFERENCE: a term minted inside this call, so no
; user value can equal it. That is how a helper says "the cell moved under me,
; re-dispatch" without a sentinel a user term could be mistaken for.

(defn force_error
  "INTERNAL: the refusal every force failure is reported as."
  [reason]
  (erlang/error (BeamLisp.LazySeq.ForceError/exception reason)))

(defn return_outcome
  "INTERNAL: peel an attempt outcome — the value, the evaluator's own failure
   replayed with `erlang:raise/3`, or the terminal reason."
  [outcome]
  (cond
    (erlang/== :completed (erlang/element 1 outcome))
      (erlang/element 2 outcome)

    (erlang/== :failed (erlang/element 1 outcome))
      (erlang/apply :erlang :raise
        (list (erlang/element 2 outcome)
              (erlang/element 3 outcome)
              (erlang/element 4 outcome)))

    :else (force_error (erlang/element 2 outcome))))

(defn attempt_outcome
  "INTERNAL: a CELL state as the OUTCOME a waiter replays. The two differ for a
   failure: the cell keeps the recipe so a retry can run it, the outcome is the
   class, the unnormalized reason and the raw stack."
  [state]
  (cond
    (erlang/== :failed (erlang/element 1 state))
      (tuple :failed (erlang/element 3 state)
             (erlang/element 4 state) (erlang/element 5 state))
    :else state))

(defn attempt_deps
  "INTERNAL: the edge policy for publishing `state` — `:keep` for a terminal
   state (it reaches nothing new), the state's own walk otherwise, which the NIF
   UNIONS with the cell's existing edges."
  [state]
  (cond
    (erlang/== :terminal (erlang/element 1 state)) :keep
    :else (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/dependencies state)))

(defn attempt_owner?
  "INTERNAL: is `state` the running attempt `attempt` owned by `owner`?"
  [state attempt owner]
  (and (erlang/is_tuple state)
       (erlang/== :running (erlang/element 1 state))
       (erlang/== owner (erlang/element 2 state))
       (erlang/== attempt (erlang/element 3 state))))

(defn attempt_notifications
  "INTERNAL: one `{:lazy_attempt, identity, attempt, outcome}` per waiter. No
   waiters is the common case and answers without minting an identity. `outcome`
   is the attempt's outcome — or `:redispatch`, the halt release's instruction to
   re-read a cell that is claimable again."
  [resource attempt waiters outcome]
  (cond
    (erlang/== waiters ()) ()
    :else
    (let [identity (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/id resource)]
      (Enum/map waiters
        (fn [w] (tuple w (tuple :lazy_attempt identity attempt outcome)))))))

(defn publish_attempt
  "INTERNAL: publish `state` over the attempt this process owns, waking every
   registered waiter with the outcome. Returns the OUTCOME; the caller peels it
   with `return_outcome`.

   An evaluator cannot be interrupted between publication and notification: both
   are one native operation, so a waiter that arrives after a successful publish
   still reads its exact answer.

   ADMITTED (PLAN-154 B): running → completed/failed can GROW the cell, so the
   publication reserves its growth atomically against the cache ceiling. A
   REFUSAL MUST NOT STRAND THE ATTEMPT — the owner holds a running cell and may
   have registered waiters — so the refusal is turned into a TERMINAL
   `{:lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget}` state instead: a constant-size control
   state replacing the constant-size running one, which grows nothing, is
   admitted at any budget, and wakes every waiter with the same reason the owner
   replays. The budget refusal is therefore terminal for the cell: it produced
   no value, and a later force fails fast with the same reason rather than
   re-running a producer that already ran."
  [l attempt state]
  (let [budget (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/cache_budget)
        resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
        version (erlang/element 1 current)
        cell (erlang/element 2 current)]
    (cond
      (attempt_owner? cell attempt (erlang/self))
      (let [waiters (erlang/element 4 cell)
            outcome (attempt_outcome state)
            outgoing (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version_admitted
                       resource version state (attempt_deps state)
                       (attempt_notifications resource attempt waiters outcome)
                       budget)]
        (cond
          (erlang/== :ok outgoing) outcome
          (erlang/== :retry outgoing) (publish_attempt l attempt state)
          (erlang/== :over_budget outgoing)
            (publish_attempt l attempt
              (tuple :terminal (tuple :lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget)))
          :else (publish_attempt l attempt (tuple :terminal :cyclic_memo))))

      (and (erlang/is_tuple cell) (erlang/== :terminal (erlang/element 1 cell)))
      cell

      :else (force_error (tuple :stale_force_owner attempt)))))

(defn evaluate_attempt
  "INTERNAL: run the recipe and publish what it produced — its value, or the
   failure `catch-raw` recorded bit for bit."
  [l attempt thunk]
  (return_outcome
    (publish_attempt l attempt
      (try
        (tuple :completed (erlang/apply thunk (list)))
        (catch-raw [kind reason stack]
          (tuple :failed thunk kind reason stack))))))

(defn capture_attempt
  "INTERNAL: run `thunk` and answer `{:ok value}` or
   `{:failed kind reason stack}`.

   The failure is captured with `catch-raw`, NOT a plain `catch`: a cell that
   must REPLAY a failure has to keep the class atom, the UNNORMALIZED reason and
   the raw frame list, and a plain `catch` rewrites exactly those (`:error`
   becomes an exception struct, a non-exception throw an ExInfo). This is the
   one seam the fold path needs from the branch's clause, so a fold's failure is
   recorded and re-raised bit for bit."
  [thunk]
  (try
    (tuple :ok (erlang/apply thunk (list)))
    (catch-raw [kind reason stack]
      (tuple :failed kind reason stack))))

(defn complete_attempt
  "INTERNAL: publish the COMPLETE element list a claimed fold produced as the
   node's shared `{:completed …}` answer, waking every waiter with it.

   Answers `:ok`; a publication the ceiling refuses RAISES the refusal, after
   `publish_attempt` has left the cell in the constant-size TERMINAL budget
   state — so a later read fails fast with the same reason instead of re-running
   a producer that already ran. The empty list publishes as nil, exactly as
   `publish_folded` did."
  [l attempt elems]
  (let [outcome (publish_attempt l attempt
                  (tuple :completed (cond (erlang/== elems ()) nil :else elems)))]
    (cond
      (erlang/== :completed (erlang/element 1 outcome)) :ok
      :else (return_outcome outcome))))

(defn fail_attempt
  "INTERNAL: publish a claimed fold's FAILURE — the node's ORIGINAL producer
   `thunk` with the class, unnormalized reason and raw frames `catch-raw`
   recorded — and re-raise it to this caller.

   Every registered waiter is woken with the same `{:failed kind reason stack}`
   outcome and replays it, so one consumer's failure reaches all of them
   unchanged; the cell keeps the producer thunk, so a later force RETRIES it,
   which is the recalled retryable-failure contract. Never returns: the raise IS
   the fold's own failure."
  [l attempt thunk kind reason stack]
  (return_outcome
    (publish_attempt l attempt (tuple :failed thunk kind reason stack))))

(defn release_recipe
  "INTERNAL: hand a claimed fold cell back UNPUBLISHED — the halt path: the
   recipe stopped at a `Reduced`, so only a prefix exists and nothing may be
   published as the node's answer.

   `pending` is the state `claim_recipe` replaced; restoring it is what keeps
   `pending?` — and a later full fold — working exactly as before the claim.
   Every registered waiter is woken with `:redispatch`: the cell is claimable
   again, so a waiter re-reads it and claims it itself instead of waiting for an
   answer that is not coming. `wait_for_attempt` maps that outcome onto its
   caller's fresh re-dispatch reference.

   THE RESTORE IS ADMITTED. Restoring a measured `{:pending thunk recipe}` over
   the constant-size running state GROWS the cell, so it is reserved like any
   other publication; it undoes a claim whose own commit had released exactly
   those bytes, so the claim/release pair is retained-neutral. A refusal turns
   the cell into the constant-size TERMINAL budget state instead (the same
   un-strand `publish_attempt` uses) rather than stranding every future consumer
   on a `:running` cell whose owner has already departed."
  [l attempt pending]
  (let [budget (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/cache_budget)
        resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
        version (erlang/element 1 current)
        cell (erlang/element 2 current)]
    (cond
      (attempt_owner? cell attempt (erlang/self))
      (let [outgoing (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version_admitted
                       resource version pending :keep
                       (attempt_notifications resource attempt
                                              (erlang/element 4 cell) :redispatch)
                       budget)]
        (cond
          (erlang/== :ok outgoing) :ok
          (erlang/== :retry outgoing) (release_recipe l attempt pending)
          (erlang/== :over_budget outgoing)
          (publish_attempt l attempt (tuple :terminal (tuple :lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget)))
          :else (publish_attempt l attempt (tuple :terminal :cyclic_memo))))

      (and (erlang/is_tuple cell) (erlang/== :terminal (erlang/element 1 cell)))
      cell

      :else (force_error (tuple :stale_force_owner attempt)))))

(defn claim_attempt
  "INTERNAL: claim the pending (or failed) state at `version` and run it.

   The claim swaps the VERSION and keeps the edges: the cell was created with the
   recipe's own captured resources as its edges, so `:keep` is exact — no
   dependency walk, no graph lock, no term comparison. The running state does not
   carry the recipe: its owner is executing it.

   `admit!` refuses BEFORE the thunk runs; the claim publication itself is
   admitted too (PLAN-154 B), because pending → running can GROW the cell (a
   small recipe against the constant 64-byte running state) — so a refusal here
   still happens before any side effect."
  [l version thunk again]
  (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/admit!)
  (let [budget (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/cache_budget)
        resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        attempt (erlang/make_ref)
        running (tuple :running (erlang/self) attempt ())
        outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version_admitted
                  resource version running :keep budget)]
    (cond
      (erlang/== :ok outcome) (evaluate_attempt l attempt thunk)
      (erlang/== :retry outcome) again
      (erlang/== :over_budget outcome)
        (force_error (tuple :lazy_cache_budget_exceeded budget))
      :else (force_error :cyclic_memo))))

(defn owner_lost
  "INTERNAL: the evaluator is gone. Publish a TERMINAL reply to every registered
   waiter so no waiter hangs on a dead owner; the reply rides the same native
   operation as the commit.

   NOT ADMITTED, deliberately: running → terminal replaces one constant 64-byte
   control state with another, so it grows NOTHING (see `estimate_bytes/1`) and
   admission could never refuse it. Gating it would only add a way for a waiter
   to be stranded by a resource verdict."
  [l owner attempt]
  (let [resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
        version (erlang/element 1 current)
        cell (erlang/element 2 current)]
    (cond
      (attempt_owner? cell attempt owner)
      (let [terminal (tuple :terminal (tuple :force_owner_lost owner attempt))
            outgoing (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version
                       resource version terminal :keep
                       (attempt_notifications resource attempt
                                              (erlang/element 4 cell) terminal))]
        (cond
          (erlang/== :ok outgoing) :ok
          (erlang/== :retry outgoing) (owner_lost l owner attempt)
          :else (force_error :cyclic_memo)))
      :else :ok)))

(defn receive_attempt
  "INTERNAL: wait for our attempt's reply, or for the owner's death.

   A receive clause's guard is a BARE `:when`, and its guard vocabulary is the
   language's own (`==`, `and`, …), never an `erlang/`-prefixed call. Both
   clauses are guarded, which is what keeps receive SELECTIVE: a message that is
   neither our attempt's reply nor our monitor's DOWN is left in the mailbox,
   exactly as the recalled module's pinned patterns did.

   Answers the RAW outcome; `wait_for_attempt` peels it, because one outcome —
   `:redispatch` — is not a value but an instruction to re-read the cell."
  [l identity owner attempt monitor]
  (receive
    [:lazy_attempt i a outcome]
    :when (and (== i identity) (== a attempt))
    (do (Process/demonitor monitor (list :flush))
        outcome)

    [:DOWN ref :process _own _reason]
    :when (== ref monitor)
    (do (owner_lost l owner attempt)
        ; A commit and its notifications are one native operation, so if the
        ; commit won before owner death its exact reply is still in flight —
        ; read it. Otherwise `owner-lost` just published ours.
        (receive
          [:lazy_attempt i2 a2 outcome2]
          :when (and (== i2 identity) (== a2 attempt))
          outcome2))))

(defn wait_for_attempt
  "INTERNAL: join a running attempt — register as a waiter with a version CAS
   (edges kept: a waiter is not a dependency), monitor the owner, and wait.

   NOT ADMITTED, for the same reason as `owner_lost`: running → running replaces
   one constant 64-byte control state with another (the waiter list is charged
   the constant, not its length), so it grows nothing and a refusal could only
   strand the waiter."
  [l owner attempt again]
  (let [resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        identity (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/id resource)
        monitor (Process/monitor owner)
        current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
        version (erlang/element 1 current)
        cell (erlang/element 2 current)]
    (cond
      (attempt_owner? cell attempt owner)
      (let [outcome (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/exchange_version
                      resource version
                      (tuple :running owner attempt
                             (erlang/++ (list (erlang/self))
                                        (erlang/element 4 cell)))
                      :keep)]
        (cond
          (erlang/== :ok outcome)
            (let [reply (receive_attempt l identity owner attempt monitor)]
              ; a released (halted) fold puts the cell back in play; the waiter
              ; re-dispatches instead of waiting for an answer that is not coming
              (cond (erlang/== reply :redispatch) again
                    :else (return_outcome reply)))
          (erlang/== :retry outcome)
            (do (Process/demonitor monitor (list :flush)) again)
          :else
            (do (Process/demonitor monitor (list :flush))
                (force_error :cyclic_memo))))
      :else (do (Process/demonitor monitor (list :flush)) again))))

(defn force_state
  "INTERNAL: the force state machine — a TOP-LEVEL self-recursion, so every
   re-dispatch after a lost race is a direct call. `again` is the caller's fresh
   reference (see the section header)."
  [l again]
  (let [resource (erlang/map_get :resource l)
        current (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read_versioned resource)
        version (erlang/element 1 current)
        state (erlang/element 2 current)
        tag (cond (erlang/is_tuple state) (erlang/element 1 state) :else state)]
    (cond
      (erlang/== tag :completed) (erlang/element 2 state)
      (erlang/== tag :terminal) (return_outcome state)

      (erlang/== tag :running)
      (cond
        (erlang/== (erlang/element 2 state) (erlang/self))
          (return_outcome (publish_attempt l (erlang/element 3 state)
                                           (tuple :terminal :recursive_force)))
        :else
          (let [r (wait_for_attempt l (erlang/element 2 state) (erlang/element 3 state) again)]
            (cond (erlang/== r again) (force_state l again) :else r)))

      (erlang/== tag :pending)
      (let [r (claim_attempt l version (erlang/element 2 state) again)]
        (cond (erlang/== r again) (force_state l again) :else r))

      (erlang/== tag :failed)
      (let [r (claim_attempt l version (erlang/element 2 state) again)]
        (cond (erlang/== r again) (force_state l again) :else r))

      :else (force_error (tuple :stale_force_owner state)))))

(defn force
  "Force a node to its value: the shared answer if one is published, the
   evaluator's own failure replayed if the attempt failed, `ForceError`
   otherwise. Concurrent forces of one node share one attempt; the waiter is told
   the exact outcome, and the owner's death is terminal.

   Every transition is a VERSION compare-and-swap, and the `deps_mode` it passes
   is the point: `:keep` for a claim, a waiter registration and a terminal state
   (they reach nothing the cell did not already hold, so no dependency is walked
   and no cycle is checked), and the state's own walk for a `{:completed v}` or
   `{:failed …}`, which the NIF UNIONS with the cell's existing edges."
  [l]
  (if (lazy? l)
    (force_state l (erlang/make_ref))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :force)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn memo_state
  "The cell's state in the LOGICAL shape callers use. `{:pending thunk}`,
   `{:completed value}`, `{:failed thunk kind reason stack}` and
   `{:terminal reason}` are the cell's own states, returned as they are. A
   running cell answers `{:running nil owner attempt waiters}`: the shape is the
   recalled one — a waiter list, an owner, an attempt — but the thunk slot is
   `nil` because the recipe is on the owner's stack by then, not on the cell.
   `Deferred`'s `realized?` reads only the `:completed` tag."
  [l]
  (if (lazy? l)
    (let [state (BeamLisp.LazyMemo/read (erlang/map_get :resource l))]
      (if (and (erlang/is_tuple state)
               (erlang/== :running (erlang/element 1 state)))
        (tuple :running nil (erlang/element 2 state)
               (erlang/element 3 state) (erlang/element 4 state))
        state))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :memo_state)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

; ── realization ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn normalize_cell
  "INTERNAL: nil for the empty list, else the list itself — the realized value
   an empty or non-empty collection normalizes to."
  [xs]
  (cond (erlang/== xs ()) nil :else xs))

(defn tagged?
  "INTERNAL: a map tagged with the struct `name` (`:__struct__` is the tag)."
  [v name]
  (and (erlang/is_map v)
       (erlang/== name (maps/get :__struct__ v nil))))

(defn realize_step
  "INTERNAL: one realize step — force, then normalize the head. TOP LEVEL so a
   nested lazy chain (`(lazy-seq (concat …))` hands back another node) is peeled
   by a direct self-call instead of an indirect one."
  [node depth]
  (if (erlang/> depth 100000)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.RuntimeError/exception
        (erlang/iolist_to_binary
          (list "LazySeq.realize: thunk chain "
                (Integer/to_string depth)
                " deep without a head — a self-referential lazy seq never produces a cell; it would hang forever"))))
    (let [v (force node)]
      (cond
        (erlang/== v ()) nil

        (tagged? v :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Vector") (normalize_cell (BeamLisp.Vector/to_list v))

        (tagged? v :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Set") (normalize_cell (BeamLisp.Set/to_list v))

        (lazy? v) (realize_step v (erlang/+ depth 1))

        (tagged? v :"Elixir.BeamLisp.SeqCursor") (BeamLisp.SeqCursor/cell v)

        :else v))))

(defn realize
  "Force a node and normalize to a realized cell: `nil` or `[head | tail]`.

   A lazy-seq body may hand back any seqable rather than a cons cell — jank
   writes `(lazy-seq c1)` around a bare collection — so normalize it here, at the
   one place a thunk's value enters the walk. The invariant every walk relies on
   is: forcing a node yields `nil` or a `[h | t]` cell, never a bare LazySeq —
   and Clojure `lazy-seq` bodies routinely return another seq. Only the HEAD is
   normalized; the tail stays lazy, so an infinite seq still realizes one cell at
   a time."
  [l]
  (if (lazy? l)
    (realize_step l 0)
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :realize)
              (tuple :arity 1))))))

(defn cell
  "Normalize any seqable to a realized cell: `nil`, a `[head | tail]` list
   (whose tail may be a lazy node), or, for non-seqables, the value itself.
   A string is seqable (its graphemes); anything else Enumerable becomes a
   cell, and a non-seqable passes through. The one chokepoint every seq walk
   (forcing, `first`, `rest`, `take`, `count`, `Enum`) goes through."
  [x]
  (cond
    (erlang/== x nil) nil
    (erlang/== x ()) nil
    (lazy? x) (realize x)
    (tagged? x :"Elixir.BeamLisp.SeqCursor") (BeamLisp.SeqCursor/cell x)
    (erlang/is_list x) x
    (tagged? x :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Vector") (normalize_cell (BeamLisp.Vector/to_list x))
    (tagged? x :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Set") (normalize_cell (BeamLisp.Set/to_list x))
    (erlang/is_binary x) (normalize_cell (String/graphemes x))
    :else (let [impl (Enumerable/impl_for x)]
            (if (erlang/== impl nil) x (normalize_cell (Enum/to_list x))))))

(defn to_list
  "Fully realize into a proper list. Iterative, so a 100k `doall` cannot blow
   the stack."
  [lazy]
  (let [step
        (fn step [c acc]
          (if (erlang/== c nil)
            (Enum/reverse acc)
            (step (cell (erlang/tl c)) (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/raw-cons (erlang/hd c) acc))))]
    (step (cell lazy) ())))

(defn prefix
  "Realize up to `n` elements, iteratively. `n` must be an integer."
  [lazy n]
  (if (erlang/is_integer n)
    (let [step
          (fn step [c k acc]
            (cond
              (<= k 0) (Enum/reverse acc)
              (erlang/== c nil) (Enum/reverse acc)
              :else (step (cell (erlang/tl c)) (erlang/- k 1)
                          (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/raw-cons (erlang/hd c) acc))))]
      (step (cell lazy) n ()))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :prefix)
              (tuple :arity 2))))))

(defn sample
  "`{taken, truncated?}`: sample up to `n` elements, flag if more remain."
  [lazy n]
  (let [taken (prefix lazy (erlang/+ n 1))]
    (if (erlang/> (erlang/length taken) n)
      (tuple (Enum/take taken n) true)
      (tuple taken false))))

(defn count
  "Element count, forcing the whole seq, iteratively."
  [lazy]
  (let [step
        (fn step [c acc]
          (if (erlang/== c nil) acc (step (cell (erlang/tl c)) (erlang/+ acc 1))))]
    (step (cell lazy) 0)))

(defn nth
  "Element at `i`, or `nil`. `i` must be a non-negative integer."
  [lazy i]
  (if (and (erlang/is_integer i) (erlang/>= i 0))
    (let [step
          (fn step [c k]
            (cond
              (erlang/== c nil) nil
              (erlang/== k 0) (erlang/hd c)
              :else (step (cell (erlang/tl c)) (erlang/- k 1))))]
      (step (cell lazy) i))
    (erlang/error
      (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
        (list (tuple :module (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))
              (tuple :function :nth)
              (tuple :arity 2))))))

(defn run
  "Force the whole seq for side effects, discarding elements."
  [lazy]
  (let [step (fn step [c] (if (erlang/== c nil) :ok (step (cell (erlang/tl c)))))]
    (step (cell lazy))))
